From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 04:27:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 349/353] soc: apple: s2r: take aura's warm boot mailbox on
 T8132

On M4 and later, iBoot locks every core's reset vector to the boot
object, so a wake restarts each core inside the boot loader, which has
to send it back to Linux. m1n1 publishes /chosen/asahi,s2r-mailbox for
that. aura, booted as a Mach-O boot object, publishes its own instead,
/chosen/aurora,s2r-mailbox (aurora-silicon/aura 30896625, "kboot: let
an OS at EL2 return from its own sleep"): 32 bytes of magic "AURAS2R1",
the boot core's MPIDR, the resume address and a resume count. The boot
core jumps to the resume address at EL2 with the MMU and caches off,
or reboots if it is 0; secondaries go back to their spin table release
address; and aura's reset paths print nothing once the mailbox is out.

Take aura's mailbox when m1n1's is absent:

- apple-s2r checks its magic and that it is for the boot CPU. aura
  names no reset vector, as it publishes the mailbox only once the
  locked RVBARs lead into its image, so the vector is the one the
  online cores have locked, which must be the same on all of them and
  is checked again before each sleep, as for m1n1.
- A T8132 sleep arms the resume address with the T8132 resume stub,
  which restarts WD2 before cpu_resume, and cleans it to the PoC. The
  wake counts a resume when aura's count moved, and clears the
  address. aura takes no warm boot flags.
- aura's mailbox is refused outside the T8132 sequence.
- apple_cpu treats aura's re-entry path as quiet, so CPU clusters can
  be power gated under it as under m1n1.

With m1n1 nothing changes: its mailbox is still looked up first.

tools/asahi/s2r-aura-mailbox-tests.py checks the layout and magic
against aura's, the lookup order, the probe checks, the arming, the
wake and the refusal.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
